<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
        user_profile.avatar_ref — the OPAQUE, STABLE, PER-USER handle under which the
        profile picture is served to OTHER users:
        GET /bff/user/character/avatar/{ref}/body.

        Why a handle at all. 022 gave the avatar bytes a serve route, but an
        OWNER-SCOPED one (GET /bff/user/profile/avatar/body resolves the owner from the
        JWT subject, so the URL is the same for everybody). That is right for "show me
        my own picture" and USELESS for the VN forum sprite, which renders the AUTHOR's
        image inside every READER's browser: the same URL in a reader's <img> would
        return the READER's avatar, not the author's. hugmesh-forum-ui therefore stored
        the placeholder "/bff/user/character/avatar" in the forum VN profile
        (hugmesh-forum-bff-api ForumVnProfile.character_img_ref) — a path with no route
        behind it at all, so every avatar-mode VN profile rendered a broken image
        (hugmesh-forum-bff-api issue #3).

        The owner cannot travel in the URL either: a Casdoor subject is frequently a
        `provider:handle` composite carrying an e-mail (`google:alice@example.com`),
        which is neither URL-path-safe nor something to publish into every reader's
        access logs — and the forum-bff allowlist that guards the stored value
        (`^/bff/user/(?!.*\.\.)[A-Za-z0-9._/-]{1,246}$`) has no `:` or `@` in its
        charset by design.

        So: a random UUID, minted ONCE per owner (lazily, the first time the owner asks
        for their own ref) and never rotated — a stored ref must keep resolving for as
        long as the VN profile holds it. It identifies the OWNER's avatar slot, not one
        upload, so re-uploading a picture keeps the same ref and simply changes the
        pixels behind it (which is also why the serve route is `no-store`).

        Column (NULLABLE — a profile with no picture has no ref, and pre-023 rows mint
        theirs on first use):
          • avatar_ref — 36-char UUID text (lowercase hex + hyphens, so it fits the
                         forum-bff allowlist charset unchanged). UNIQUE, so the reverse
                         lookup ref -> owner can never be ambiguous; the index makes
                         that lookup a point read. Nullable + UNIQUE is fine on both
                         Postgres and the H2 PostgreSQL-compat slice (NULLs do not
                         collide).

        Numbered 023 — the band ABOVE the current max (022-user-profile-avatar-body),
        registered LAST in db.changelog-master.xml with a SINGLE <include>.
    -->

    <changeSet id="023-1-user-profile-avatar-ref" author="hugmesh">
        <addColumn tableName="user_profile">
            <column name="avatar_ref" type="varchar(36)"/>
        </addColumn>
        <createIndex tableName="user_profile" indexName="ux_user_profile_avatar_ref" unique="true">
            <column name="avatar_ref"/>
        </createIndex>
        <rollback>
            <dropIndex tableName="user_profile" indexName="ux_user_profile_avatar_ref"/>
            <dropColumn tableName="user_profile" columnName="avatar_ref"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
